    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43479.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "This is such an important question, Matt. It's really we need to unpack it because it can get complicated. In a campaign, when a Republican runs for Congress, one of the first things that the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee does is some basic research. Usually this is done by a research intern. In many cases, it doesn't even rise to somebody in a more substantive role. And so in most cases, an intern in the research department of the DCCC will do the equivalent of a Lexus-Nexus search. You wanna know, does this Republican running against Paul Hodes any tax liens, any j-walking tickets, any moving violations, any nasty divorces. What's out there in the public record that we're going to want to know about? Now, that usually will, look, in many cases, it's rather banal, right? But in some cases, you get some red flags. At that point, it will go to a more serious investigation. Now, you kick the tires and if you see something, Well, then you want to look under the hood. And in that case, in many respects, a research staffer will prepare a more thorough research book. That will then go to the actual campaign. Now, here was a deficiency in the Democratic campaign against Santos in my view. The Deepsea had a research project. They said to Mr. Zimmerman, look, there are some red flags here. Mr.",
