    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think I've actually heard a number that kind of quantifies how close they got. And some people say, well, they literally had about 40 to 50% of what ultimately broke. But I have to say, I'm not sure that even at the additional 10, 20, 30, 40% I'm not quite sure that it would have changed the trajectory of the media's interest in this and this is the big story here is just that complacency sleepy congressional district that generally votes for Democrats it's purple not unlike hoses old district. generally vote for Democrats, but the Republican would be okay, as long as the Republican is moderate. Partisanship is not infused in the DNA in this district, right? So kind of a sleepy complacent district. And then you have this conventional wisdom that George Santos is just not going to win. This isn't a story, he's going away. And so why spend resources on this? And then factor into this, the third, and I think the most serious point for the future of our democracy. And that is the consolidation of suburban newspapers. So as newspapers have consolidated, and so many have just gone out of business, and stress has been put on the budgets of local newsrooms, and investigative journalists have been laid off, or their bandwidth is just really narrow. The result is that you don't get the kind of scrutiny that you used to when you see these red flags. Put it all to get you have a perfect storm of complacency, which led to the storm of George Santos being sworn in. And now everybody's saying, how did we miss it? Well, it's quite clear how it was missed.",
